{
  "title": "The bound-choice enum is built per request, but one response can answer many decisions -- calls 2..N carry the previous decision's menu",
  "description": "Measured in the live schema eval (guarded arm, job 9970, ~150 of 200 games): on the decision \
each enum was BUILT FOR, 0 off-menu ids in ~8,000 bound answers. The grammar holds. But 25 off-menu ids \
were still produced UNDER A PRESENT TAG, and every one came from the same place: a response containing \
MORE THAN ONE `choose_action` call. MECHANISM: pilot.py assembles one request per LLM call and binds it \
to the single decision in hand (`decision_blob_for_binding`), so the structural tag's enum is that \
decision's menu. The model is free to emit several tool calls in one response. The first answers the \
decision the enum was built for. It is executed, the game advances to a NEW decision with a DIFFERENT \
menu, and calls 2..N are then executed against that new decision while still carrying ids constrained \
to the OLD menu -- which is precisely an off-menu id (`Object pN not found in current choices`). \
EVIDENCE: 34 choose_action calls in the guarded arm have no coverage row of their own; 30 are \
immediately preceded by another choose_action, and all 34 came from responses holding 2, 10 or 12 \
choose_action calls (14 / 9 / 11 respectively). 25 of the 34 are off-menu -- 73.5%, against 0.00% on \
calls that had their own coverage row. One example response emits `{\"choice\": \"p20\"}` twice. WHY IT \
MATTERS: it is the only source of off-menu ids on the guarded arm outside the decision classes the rule \
declines by design, so it is the entire residual defect of the mechanism -- small in count, total in \
kind. It also means the guarded arm's off-menu total understates what binding alone achieves, and a \
reader comparing totals would attribute to the enum a failure the enum did not have. The 10- and \
12-call responses are themselves a loop mode worth its own look. SUGGESTED FIX, either: (a) constrain \
the grammar to AT MOST ONE choose_action per response when the enum is bound, so the tag cannot \
authorise answers to decisions it was not built for; or (b) execute only the first choose_action of a \
bound response and return the rest to the model with the new decision attached, so each later answer \
is re-bound. (a) is smaller and fails closed; (b) preserves throughput. Either way the fix is TESTED \
by the readout's own class 'extra call in a multi-call response', which must go to zero. NOT FIXED: the \
eval arms are running on the current behaviour and must finish on identical bytes; this is for after \
the readout. Found by the readout's order-join, which attributes each answer to the coverage row that \
preceded it -- a choose_action with no row of its own was the anomaly that exposed it.",
  "priority": "p2",
